+# Protectorate Ship, Fleet, and Regional Response Formation Atlas
+
+## 1. Purpose
+
+This atlas defines Protectorate spacecraft and formations as **protective infrastructure with combat capability**, not conventional navy classes renamed with benevolent language.
+
+A vessel or formation is distinguished by:
+
+- what it can shelter;
+- what it can repair;
+- how quickly it can reach danger;
+- what force it can safely apply;
+- how long it can sustain civilians and responders;
+- what authority it can lawfully coordinate;
+- what happens after violence ends.
+
+# 2. Common Protectorate Ship Baseline
+
+Every major Protectorate vessel should retain meaningful capacity for:
+
+- habitation;
+- medical care;
+- rescue;
+- fabrication and repair;
+- logistics;
+- communications;
+- evidence preservation;
+- training;
+- diplomacy;
+- evacuation;
+- proportionate defense.
+
+Mission emphasis changes allocation, not personhood or civic baseline.
+
+# 3. Ship Families
+
+## 3.1 Bastionline Protectorate Carrier
+
+**Role envelope:** escort, rescue, command, repair, protected habitation, sustained defense.
+
+**Architecture:** separated command citadels, protected harbor canyon, redundant propulsion clusters, distributed radiator fields, compartmentalized shelter districts.
+
+**Carried ecology:** interceptors, rescue tugs, hospital craft, repair pods, boarding and evidence teams, evacuation modules.
+
+**Signature gameplay:** keeping a mixed civilian convoy coherent under attack and infrastructure failure.
+
+**Risk:** emergency command becoming permanent fleet sovereignty.
+
+## 3.2 Shield-Harbor Carrier
+
+**Role envelope:** physically shelter vulnerable vessels, provide protected docking, route passage, and rapid repair.
+
+**Architecture:** open central harbor with movable shielding petals and sacrificial debris structures.
+
+**Strength:** civilian survival during bombardment, debris storms, and route collapse.
+
+**Weakness:** slow maneuvering and enormous heat burden when harbor shields are closed.
+
+**Political meaning:** welcomed as refuge, feared as a mobile customs and detention authority.
+
+## 3.3 Recovery Ark
+
+**Role envelope:** post-battle rescue, casualty care, body and source-chain recovery, evidence preservation, contamination handling.
+
+**Architecture:** large medical and quarantine districts, pale high-visibility hull, protected morgue and identity-reconstruction facilities.
+
+**Strength:** restoring personhood and records after violence.
+
+**Weakness:** limited stand-off combat and extreme political exposure to all sides.
+
+## 3.4 Route Sentinel
+
+**Role envelope:** persistent patrol, traffic protection, beacon repair, anti-piracy, search and rescue.
+
+**Architecture:** medium multirole vessel with strong sensors, long endurance, several utility bays, modest habitation.
+
+**Strength:** early presence and local relationships.
+
+**Weakness:** insufficient mass for major fleet combat without coalition support.
+
+## 3.5 Pursuit Interceptor Tender
+
+**Role envelope:** rapid response, reconnaissance, interception, remote rescue, and pursuit of threats away from civilian space.
+
+**Architecture:** compact carrier optimized around fast craft maintenance and crew recovery rather than heavy weapons.
+
+**Strength:** speed and distributed reach.
+
+**Weakness:** limited shelter capacity and dependence on supply nodes.
+
+## 3.6 Planetary Protectorate Carrier
+
+**Role envelope:** atmosphere, orbit, surface logistics, evacuation, disaster response, and limited defense.
+
+**Architecture:** orbital mothership carrying trans-atmospheric rescue craft, surface mobile bases, drones, and environmental packages.
+
+**Strength:** connecting orbital safety to embodied planetary rescue.
+
+**Weakness:** must divide resources among several media and legal jurisdictions.
+
+## 3.7 Ocean Protectorate Cradle
+
+**Role envelope:** flotilla defense, submarine rescue, storm response, marine ecology protection, coastal evacuation.
+
+**Architecture:** orbital or surface carrier with amphibious landers, submarines, floating hospitals, dive systems, and acoustic command networks.
+
+**Strength:** water-world and under-ice operations.
+
+**Weakness:** pressure-rated systems consume mass and maintenance capacity.
+
+## 3.8 Debris Shepherd Fleet Tender
+
+**Role envelope:** orbital cleanup, wreck stabilization, collision avoidance, salvage arbitration, disabled-vessel rescue.
+
+**Architecture:** open truss ship with tug berths, material recovery systems, tracking arrays, and external work habitats.
+
+**Strength:** preventing the battlefield from remaining lethal for centuries.
+
+**Weakness:** vulnerable during extended external operations.
+
+## 3.9 Boundary Translation Vessel
+
+**Role envelope:** first-contact safety, quarantine, translation, ritual compliance, diplomatic extraction.
+
+**Architecture:** reconfigurable environmental interfaces, multisensory communication spaces, several independent diplomatic habitats.
+
+**Strength:** preventing ontological misunderstanding from becoming war.
+
+**Weakness:** slower command cycle and high dependence on rare specialists.
+
+## 3.10 Siege-Relief Carrier
+
+**Role envelope:** break blockades, deliver food and medicine, evacuate civilians, restore utilities, defend corridors.
+
+**Architecture:** heavily redundant cargo and repair systems, distributed defensive weapons, hardened landing and transfer craft.
+
+**Strength:** enduring concentrated danger while continuing service.
+
+**Weakness:** easily reinterpreted as an offensive siege-breaker and politically destabilizing.
+
+# 4. Small Craft Ecology
+
+Protectorate ships rely on many small craft:
+
+- rescue skiffs;
+- medevac shuttles;
+- debris interceptors;
+- repair tugs;
+- boarding and inspection craft;
+- firefighting pods;
+- atmospheric dropships;
+- ocean rescue vehicles;
+- contamination capsules;
+- evidence couriers;
+- mobile shelters;
+- sensor pickets;
+- escort drones;
+- surrender reception craft.
+
+Small craft should have persistent crews, repair histories, and local cultures. They are not expendable ammunition.
+
+# 5. Formation Families
+
+## 5.1 Protected convoy
+
+Contains:
+
+- civilian carriers;
+- cargo ships;
+- mobile homes;
+- hospital craft;
+- escorts;
+- rescue reserve;
+- repair tender;
+- sensor pickets.
+
+Success is measured by protected continuity, not enemy destruction.
+
+## 5.2 Disaster response group
+
+Contains:
+
+- medical carrier;
+- fabrication tender;
+- evacuation ships;
+- ecological teams;
+- communications restoration;
+- local units.
+
+Command may be shared between technical, medical, and civic authorities.
+
+## 5.3 Route reopening group
+
+Contains:
+
+- scouts;
+- debris shepherds;
+- repair crews;
+- security escorts;
+- archive and evidence teams;
+- diplomatic liaison.
+
+The route is not reopened until it is safe and politically usable, not merely cleared of enemies.
+
+## 5.4 Hostile-world recovery group
+
+Contains:
+
+- survival carrier;
+- quarantine vessel;
+- scientific and translation teams;
+- rescue craft;
+- armed reserve;
+- remote probes.
+
+Primary objective may shift from rescue to containment or withdrawal.
+
+## 5.5 Blockade relief coalition
+
+Contains:
+
+- Siege-Relief carrier;
+- convoy ships;
+- escort units;
+- medical reserve;
+- negotiation vessel;
+- public witness and evidence teams.
+
+Its greatest challenge is preserving civilian purpose while under military pressure.
+
+## 5.6 Planetary evacuation lattice
+
+Not one fleet, but a network of:
+
+- orbital carriers;
+- atmospheric shuttles;
+- surface convoys;
+- shelters;
+- ports;
+- hospitals;
+- registries;
+- receiving settlements.
+
+This formation is defined by flows, not a single commander.
+
+# 6. Formation Command Culture
+
+Different formations may use:
+
+- lead vessel command;
+- rotating mission coordination;
+- distributed intent;
+- profession-specific authority;
+- consensus between autonomous units;
+- machine vote or proof systems;
+- alien ritual timing.
+
+The fleet UI must show where authority actually resides.
+
+# 7. Combat Doctrine
+
+Protectorate doctrine should prioritize:
+
+1. detect and identify;
+2. warn and de-escalate;
+3. move civilians and vulnerable systems;
+4. deny attack paths;
+5. intercept threats;
+6. disable where feasible;
+7. destroy when necessary;
+8. rescue and recover;
+9. preserve evidence;
+10. restore passage and ordinary life.
+
+This order may compress under immediate attack but remains the institutional ideal.
+
+# 8. Weapons and Constraints
+
+Protectorate vessels may use:
+
+- point defense;
+- interceptors;
+- electronic warfare;
+- directed energy;
+- kinetic interception;
+- missiles;
+- mines or area-denial systems under strict law;
+- boarding teams;
+- metric or alien defensive technologies.
+
+Every weapon requires:
+
+- power;
+- cooling;
+- ammunition or reaction mass;
+- sensor confidence;
+- firing geometry;
+- safe exclusion zones;
+- command authorization;
+- collateral prediction;
+- post-use evidence.
+
+# 9. Peace-Time Life
+
+Protectorate vessels spend much of their lives on:
+
+- training;
+- rescue;
+- inspections;
+- route maintenance;
+- escort;
+- debris clearance;
+- disaster response;
+- public education;
+- joint exercises;
+- ship repair;
+- political negotiation.
+
+Residents maintain schools, kitchens, relationships, art, worship, arguments, and ordinary work. A Protectorate carrier is not permanently at battle stations.
+
+# 10. Failure and Damage Culture
+
+Ships should fail according to architecture:
+
+- harbor collision;
+- radiator puncture;
+- propulsion separation;
+- command partition;
+- medical overload;
+- shelter crowding;
+- contaminated rescue craft;
+- interceptor losses;
+- communication deception;
+- civilian mutiny against prolonged emergency authority.
+
+Damage changes politics and life, not only combat statistics.
+
+# 11. Factional Variants
+
+The same ship lineage may be operated by:
+
+- municipal federation;
+- worker cooperative;
+- Vast Burden House;
+- machine continuity compact;
+- ocean treaty;
+- corporate protection service;
+- authoritarian protectorate;
+- alien partner civilization.
+
+Doctrine, interiors, crew rights, and surrender behavior should reveal the operator.
+
+# 12. Player Progression
+
+Players can progress through:
+
+- rescue volunteer;
+- small-craft crew;
+- station qualification;
+- interceptor or tug captain;
+- formation planner;
+- ship department lead;
+- temporary mission coordinator;
+- carrier commander;
+- charter reformer;
+- shipyard founder.
+
+The progression fantasy is stewardship of increasing interdependence, not possession of larger guns.
+
+# 13. Concept-Art Requirements
+
+Future ship art should show:
+
+- protected civilians and carried craft;
+- obvious rescue and repair architecture;
+- distributed radiators and power;
+- habitation and ordinary life;
+- damage-control access;
+- culturally specific markings;
+- scale references;
+- weapons integrated as constrained systems rather than decorative turrets.
+
+# Closing Principle
+
+> **A Protectorate fleet should look powerful, but the reason for that power should remain visible in every harbor, hospital, shelter, repair dock, and civilian vessel traveling inside its protection.**
